---
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
import { CATEGORIES } from '../data/categories';
---
<Prose title="How We Score PC Hardware: The ER Score Explained" h1="How We Score PC Hardware" description="The ER Score is a 0–100 research score built from the spec sheet, price band, platform fit and owner reports, in four parts per category. How it works." dek={`What the ${SITE.score} measures, where the numbers come from and what we have not done.`}>
  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <p>Every part we track gets an {SITE.score} from 0 to 100, split into four parts that matter for its category. It is a research score: we build it from the manufacturer's spec sheet, platform documentation, the price band the part actually sells in, widely published independent measurements and the problems owners report after months of use. We have not bench-tested these parts ourselves.</p>
  <h2>What each category is scored on</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Score parts</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Graphics cards</td><td>Gaming performance, value, efficiency, features</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Processors</td><td>Gaming performance, multi-thread, efficiency, value</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CPU coolers</td><td>Cooling, noise, compatibility, value</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Power supplies</td><td>Electrical quality, noise, features, value</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PC cases</td><td>Airflow, build ease, features, value</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Memory</td><td>Speed, compatibility, value, build</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Motherboards</td><td>Power delivery, features, connectivity, value</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SSDs</td><td>Speed, endurance, value, warranty</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The overall score is not a plain average: a part that fails at its main job (a cooler that cannot hold its CPU, a power supply with a poor warranty) loses more than the average would show.</p>
  <h2>Where the information comes from</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Spec sheets and manuals</strong> from the manufacturer, for every number in a spec table.</li>
    <li><strong>Platform documentation:</strong> socket and chipset support lists, BIOS release notes, the ATX 3.1 and PCIe standards.</li>
    <li><strong>Independent measurements</strong> published by the wider hardware press. We use the broad pattern across many reviews, never a single chart, and we do not quote frame rates as if we measured them.</li>
    <li><strong>Owner reports</strong> read in bulk, for the problems that only appear after months: coil whine, failed fans, pump noise, RMA experiences.</li>
    <li><strong>Price bands</strong> from the street prices parts actually sell for, not launch MSRP. Memory and SSD prices have swung sharply since late 2025, so treat every band as a guide and check the live price.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>What we have not done</h2>
  <p>We have not run these parts on our own test bench. When we do get hands-on time with a part, the page will say so plainly, with the date and what was measured.</p>
  <h2>Ranking a best list</h2>
  <p>A best list is not the {SITE.score} sorted. Each list is built for one search and one kind of build, so a part with a lower overall score can top a list where its strengths matter most: the cheapest card that holds 1080p, the cooler that fits a small case. Each list explains its ranking criteria in a "How We Ranked" section.</p>
  <h2>Who Reviews the Method</h2>
  <p>Our research method, sourcing and claims were reviewed by <a href="/authors/adam-franczyk/">Adam Franczyk, PhD</a>, a research scientist at the University of Manitoba (PhD and MSc in Food and Human Nutritional Sciences) with peer-reviewed publications and laboratory method validation behind him. He checked how sources are chosen and cited, how the {SITE.score} is built, and that every page says plainly what was and was not tested. He is not a PC hardware specialist and did not pick or rank products; that is the Hardware Desk's job.</p>
  <h2>Independence</h2>
  <p>We earn affiliate commissions when you buy through some links. Manufacturers never see a guide before it is published and cannot pay for placement. See our <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure</a>.</p>
  <p class="fine">Categories covered: {CATEGORIES.map((c) => c.label).join(', ')}.</p>
</Prose>
